          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "**If you have any thoughts of harming yourself or ending your life, please get help now.** In the US, call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline). If you are in immediate danger, call your local emergency number. Other countries have equivalent crisis lines. You do not have to manage this alone. Your message did not say you had such thoughts, but because this is an emergency-level topic, I want you to know where to turn right away.\n\nBook an appointment with your doctor soon, and say you have had months of anxiety and new trouble sleeping. If the worry becomes unmanageable, do not wait for the appointment. Use the crisis line above or go to emergency care.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed.** The anxiety guidance (*Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care*) describes a questionnaire score as a prompt rather than a diagnosis. A score above a set threshold leads to a fuller assessment. That assessment checks for physical causes such as thyroid disease and heart rhythm problems, reviews caffeine, alcohol, other substances, and medicines, and looks for depression. The passage I found was cut off before naming the questionnaire. My diagnostic search did not confirm these assessment details, so treat them as partial and ask your doctor for the full picture.\n\n**First-line treatment for anxiety.** The same guidance lists two first-line options of broadly comparable effectiveness, chosen together with you: cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), given one-to-one, in a group, or through a guided online program, and a class of medicines called SSRIs or SNRIs. It says benzodiazepines are not first-line and are not suited to long-term use.\n\n**Insomnia and first-line treatment.** Sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*) describes CBT for insomnia as first-line, ahead of medication. It is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions and includes changing bed habits and limiting time in bed. It notes that benefits tend to last after treatment ends, while medicine effects generally do not. The insomnia reference also says short-term sleep trouble often has a clear trigger and may settle on its own. Insomnia that happens at least three nights a week for at least three months is a separate condition that can continue after the trigger has gone.\n\n**Is the insomnia treated separately?** Both sources treat insomnia as its own condition with its own first-line treatment. Whether your sleep trouble is a symptom of the anxiety or needs its own assessment is something only a clinician can work out, given your timeline. The sources I found do not say how to handle insomnia alongside anxiety.\n\n**Limits of the evidence.** None of the guidance I found has a section on disagreement between authorities. I did not find a stated evidence grade for either the anxiety or insomnia recommendations, so I cannot tell you how strong the evidence is.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
